  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper-level low will continue to slowly drift eastward over\nthe region through tomorrow while multiple shortwaves rotate\ncyclonically around the low. This, combined with the presence \nof a cold pool aloft will allow for isolated to widely scattered\nshowers to continue through the rest of today and into \ntomorrow. Weak instability could allow for a rumble of thunder \nas well but overall coverage of this looks rather sparse. Weak \nshear should prevent any storms from becoming severe. Any \nprecipitation amounts look rather light as well so no hydrologic\nimpacts are expected. High temperatures will be in the 60s and \n70s which are a few degrees below normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWeak upper ridging and surface high pressure will bring dry\nweather for Wednesday before another upper trough returns for\nthe end of the week. A surface low pressure system will slowly\ntrack across the region Thursday into Friday which will bring\nour next best chance for more widespread showers and some\nthunderstorms. Will continue to monitor trends whether or not\nstorms could be on the stronger side. Mid-level lapse rates look\nto be between 6.0 to 6.5C/km with around 25-30 kt of shear which\ncould allow for some storm development, but these conditions are \nborderline for organized severe thunderstorms. Dewpoints will \nalso rise into the mid-60s to lower 70s later in the week as \nwell. Next weekend looks drier to start with high pressure\nreturning.",
